  ' ============================================================
  ' BLACKBOARDS NOTE
  ' ============================================================

  note as BlackboardNote
    **Two Blackboards (Source + Target) of one generic class, bridged
    by a sequential SourceFreeze snapshot**

    GSM Sourcer realises the Blackboard pattern as **two instances**
    of a single generic `Blackboard` class:

    - **`SourceBlackboard`** -- owned by the Sourcing Pack(s); hosts
      the Pack-declared source-analysis panels, stratified by the
      Pack's own declared **abstraction layers**.
    - **`TargetBlackboard`** -- owned by the engine; hosts the GSM
      target spine as a **3-layer fixed stack**: `L_DEFINITION`
      (identity claims), `L_ARCHETYPE` (classification), `L_STATEMENT`
      (fully populated body).

    A single generic `Controller` class is also instantiated twice
    (`SourceController`, `TargetController`); each runs its own
    Activation -> Execution -> Conflict-Resolution loop until
    quiescence (or `runInvariants.completionPredicate` /
    `maxIterations`). The two phases run **sequentially**: Source
    quiesces, the engine **freezes** the SourceBlackboard into an
    immutable `SourceFreeze` artefact, then the TargetController
    starts. Target-phase KSs read frozen Source state via
    `controlContext.sourceFreeze()` (a `SourceFreezeView`); they
    cannot post back to the Source Blackboard.

    The Controllers remain **panel-graph-agnostic in semantics** --
    they know only the declared panel DAG of their Blackboard
    instance and each KS's Java
    `triggerPredicate(BlackboardView, SourcingRunContext)` method,
    not what any panel means.

    **One Java type for all panels.** A panel has no behaviour; it is
    metadata describing a typed region of one Blackboard. The engine
    therefore models every panel as one Java record
    `Panel` with fields `(name, blackboard, abstractionLayer, slots,
    dependsOnPanels)`, where `slots` maps each slot name to a
    `ContributionSlot`. There is no class hierarchy, no abstract
    `SourceAnalysisPanel`, no abstract `TargetDefinitionPanel`, no
    per-panel concrete subclass. Differentiation is by which
    `Blackboard` instance hosts the panel (`blackboard` back-reference);
    engine-vs-Pack ownership is derived from the blackboard
    (`TargetBlackboard` ⇒ engine; `SourceBlackboard` ⇒ Pack); the
    registration validator enforces this and rejects Pack-declared
    Target panels.

    **Boundary at registration, not at compile time.** Because Packs
    contribute panels as data (entries in `pack-manifest.panels[]`),
    the engine has zero compile-time dependency on Pack code for
    panel definitions. Pack-owned KS Java classes still load in
    sandboxed classloaders, but no Pack class extends a panel
    abstract -- the engine's `PanelRegistry` accepts a `Panel` record
    regardless of who declared it, and enforces invariants at
    registration time (`blackboard = SourceBlackboard` ⇒ `name` MUST
    be Pack-namespaced AND `abstractionLayer` MUST be one of the
    Pack's declared layer ids; `blackboard = TargetBlackboard` ⇒
    panel MUST be one of the engine's three reserved Definition /
    Archetype / Statement panels; etc.).

    ====

    **Abstraction layers (panel stratification metadata)**

    Each panel declares an `abstractionLayer` id. Layers are
    **informational stratification metadata** (visualization,
    cross-Pack reasoning); they DO NOT enforce data flow -- the panel
    DAG (`dependsOnPanels`) remains the operational truth. Convention
    across both Blackboards: **lower rank = less integrated / more
    concrete; higher rank = more integrated / closer to a usable
    answer.**

    - **Source side**: each Sourcing Pack declares its own
      `abstractionLayers[]` in its manifest (`{id, rank, description}`
      entries). Engine validates each Source panel's
      `abstractionLayer` is one of the Pack's declared layer ids.
      Recommended (NOT enforced) convention for software-repository
      Packs: rank 0 `raw` (file tree, AST tokens), rank 1 `micro`
      (functions, classes), rank 2 `meso` (modules, services), rank 3
      `macro` (bounded contexts, capabilities), rank 4 `intent`
      (responsibility, behavioural semantics).
    - **Target side**: the engine ships a fixed 3-layer enum
      `TargetLayer` = { `L_DEFINITION` (rank 0), `L_ARCHETYPE`
      (rank 1), `L_STATEMENT` (rank 2) }. Pack `extendsPanels[]`
      additive slots inherit the host panel's layer.

    ====

    **Slot ontology (closed slot sets)**

    Every `Panel` declares a closed `slots` map at
    Pack-registration time. Each entry binds a slot
    name (e.g. `Claim`, `Verdict`, `Application`, `Draft`) to the
    JSON Schema URI that validates Contributions of that type. A
    `Contribution` belongs to exactly one `(panel, slot)` pair, with
    the slot name drawn from the panel's closed map.
    Adding a slot at Run time is rejected (invariant
    I-3 in the GSM Sourcing Blackboard skill). There is NO
    panel-level value schema; schemas live one-per-slot.

    ====

    **Dropped panels (Wave 9)**

    Three panels were removed from the Target Blackboard ontology in
    Wave 9. Their semantics were not lost -- they were re-homed onto
    structures that already carried the right shape:

    - **`Granularity`** -> dropped as a panel. Per-Run policy moved
      to `RunConfig.granularityPolicy` (Pack-typed). Per-identity
      verdicts moved to a new **`Definition.Verdict`** slot, posted
      by a `GranularityKS` declared `direction = TOP_DOWN` (reads
      Statement, writes Definition.Verdict). Precedence over the
      identity-time `Definition.Merge` slot is preserved.
    - **`Evidence`** -> dropped as a panel. Replaced by an optional
      `Contribution.evidence: List<EvidenceRef>` property attached by
      any KS that has evidence to attach.
    - **`Confidence`** -> dropped as a panel. Synthesis is now done by
      `ConfidenceSynthesisKS` posting a refined Statement
      Contribution whose `confidence` value reflects the synthesis;
      folded into the resolved Statement by the `compose` resolver.
      The per-Contribution `confidence` field (already mandatory)
      carries the score.

    ====

    **Conflict resolution and the resolved view**

    Each `(panel, slot)` declares a default `ContributionResolver`.
    KSs may post multiple competing Contributions for the same
    `(panel, slot, identityKey)` triple; the resolver collapses them
    into a single resolved value visible via
    `BlackboardView.resolved(panel, identityKey)`. Resolvers:
    `highestConfidence`, `firstWriter`, `failOnConflict`,
    `collectAll`, `compose` (schema-driven per-key
    merge of value objects -- used by Statement so multiple
    StatementShapingKSs can populate disjoint archetype fields).
    `ConfidenceSynthesisKS`, `ReconciliationKS`, and ensemble KSs MUST
    use `BlackboardView.contributionsOf(panel, identityKey)` instead --
    `resolved` would already have collapsed the diversity those KSs
    need to reason over.

    ====

    **Engine run state distinct from the Blackboards**

    The Run input (operator intent, scope, focus, profile,
    granularity policy, resolver overrides, external tool bindings,
    run invariants) does NOT live on either Blackboard as a
    confidence-1.0 hypothesis (a deliberate departure from
    Hearsay-II). It lives in `SourcingRunContext` -- a **frozen
    run-scoped engine value, distinct from the Blackboards** --
    accessed via `controlContext.runContext()`. The `SourceFreeze`
    artefact (Source Blackboard's frozen state at quiescence) is
    *also* distinct from `SourcingRunContext` and is accessed via the
    separate `controlContext.sourceFreeze()` accessor by Target-phase
    KSs.

    ====

    **Panel DAG (engine-validated at Pack registration)**

    Each Blackboard has its own DAG.

    Source Blackboard (Pack-defined; example for the ITIP Repository
    Sourcing Pack):
    ```
    itip:SourceInventory  ->  itip:SourceUnit
    itip:SourceUnit       ->  itip:SourceInterface
    itip:SourceUnit       ->  itip:SourceRelation
    ```

    Bridge (sequential, engine-driven; not a DAG edge):
    ```
    SourceBlackboard --[ quiescence ]--> SourceFreeze
                     --[ TargetController start ]-->  Target KSs
    ```

    Target Blackboard (engine-fixed):
    ```
    Definition  ->  Archetype  ->  Statement       (BOTTOM_UP)
    Statement   --[ TOP_DOWN ]-->   Definition.Verdict
    ```

    A BOTTOM_UP KS's `readsPanels` MUST be a subset of the transitive
    ancestors of its `writesPanel` in the DAG of its target
    Blackboard. A TOP_DOWN KS's `readsPanels` MAY include descendants
    of `writesPanel` (sanctioned exception: `GranularityKS` reads
    Statement, writes Definition). Cross-Blackboard reads from Target
    KSs into Source panels are resolved via the `SourceFreezeView` --
    no live cross-Blackboard reads.
  end note

